    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm trying to fix my lawn in portland but it's looking kinda yellowish. can u explain how those NPK numbers on fertilizer bags actually work and what i should look for if it's a phosphorus problem?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The NPK numbers on fertilizer bags—like 10-10-10 or 20-5-10—represent the three primary macronutrients your lawn needs, and they are the percentages of Nitrogen (N), Phosphorus (P), and Potassium (K). If your lawn is looking yellowish, it's often a sign of a nutrient deficiency, which is what the fertilizer is meant to fix. A phosphorus problem, which is the middle number (P), usually manifests as stunted growth or poor root development, and you'll want to look for a fertilizer with a higher middle number. Remember, the best fertilizer for your lawn depends on what your soil is currently lacking, so a soil test is always the most reliable way to know what to apply.",
